00:00:03.379 --> 00:00:05.360 align:start position:0%
for humans monitoring vital signs is  usually just a matter of wearing a small  device on the tip of your finger or a  quick listen with the stethoscope and  you're good to go  but for small animals such as mice or  birds checking vitals can involve  shaving hair plucking feathers sometimes  even surgery and anesthesia these  procedures can be stressful for the  animals and you don't always get the  best readings now researchers have  developed a non-invasive device that can  monitor the vital signs of small animals  using either tiny antennae or RFID tags  like those used in stores to track  inventory the device uses near-field  coherent sensing to read pulse heart  rate and breathing all without touching  the animal veer field refers to the  electromagnetic field directly around an  RFID antenna this allows readings up to  7 centimeters away from the antenna in  this setup the small antenna directs  waves of electromagnetic energy towards  the animal and towards a receiver once  these waves reflect off the body the  receiver can analyze the differences  between them changes in phase are used  to detect motion like breathing or pulse  the heartbeat is signaled by changes in  amplitude to get blood pressure the  timing of the heartbeat and pulse are  compared the researchers tested the  method on a rat and found the RFID  antenna was just as accurate and  monitoring heartbeat and breathing rate  as an electrocardiogram scientists  successfully tested the device parakeets  hamsters and beta fish-- yes even  underwater as touchless sensing  technology was originally developed for  reading vital science and humans imagine  a hospital before each patient has their  own unique RFID tag sewn directly into  their hospital gown with a central  reader that can monitor up to 200 people  at once the tags could even be used to  detect other internal motions in the  body such as bowel or eye movement  researchers believe in the future this  new technology will allow scientists to  track vital signs and a wide variety of  laboratory animals bets to check on  exotic pets much more humanely so your  hamster won't have to stress over  getting a haircut just to get a reading  of his heart rate  [Music]  you  [Music]
 

